# The two Drift web assets are vendored build artifacts, not source.
#
# The committed drift_worker.js already contains CRLF line endings (355,547
# bytes, 13,363 of them), so a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf on does not
# convert LF to CRLF - it converts each existing CRLF to CRCRLF, inflating the
# working-tree copy to 368,910 bytes. The blob and the Linux CI build are
# untouched, so the app is unaffected; what breaks is the ability to verify a
# vendored binary, and nothing in the repo notices:
#
#   - test/app/web_assets_test.dart bounds sizes with greaterThan, by design, so
#     a version bump does not trip it - a 13KB inflation passes.
#   - web/DRIFT_ASSETS.md records versions and source URLs, not hashes.
#   - a byte or hash comparison against upstream, or against CI's web-build
#     artifact, therefore fails on Windows and succeeds on Linux for the same
#     commit. That comparison is how P0.6's web fix was confirmed (PR #4), and
#     it would have reported a false mismatch had it run after a fresh checkout
#     rather than before one.
#
# -text pins both files to their committed bytes on every platform, so the
# hashes in DRIFT_ASSETS.md verify anywhere and a real mismatch means a real
# problem. It does not rewrite what is committed: the blob keeps its CRLF, which
# differs from upstream's LF but is the exact byte sequence CI built and shipped
# green, and is not worth re-verifying to normalise.
web/drift_worker.js -text
web/sqlite3.wasm -text binary
